        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            Common Concrete Floor Painting Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete floor paint fails for one reason far more often than any other: it was applied to a surface it could not grip. Everything below follows from that.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Painting a Sealed or Troweled Slab</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A slab that beads water is already sealed, and paint applied over it will peel in sheets the first time a tyre turns on it. Sprinkle water on the floor: if it beads rather than darkening the concrete, the surface must be acid-etched or mechanically ground before any coating goes down.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Moisture Test</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Vapour moving up through a slab pushes coatings off from underneath, and no amount of surface prep prevents it. Tape a square of plastic sheet down for 24 to 48 hours; condensation on the underside means you need a moisture-tolerant system, not standard floor paint.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Interior Latex Because It Is Cheaper</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ordinary latex has no abrasion resistance and no chemical resistance, so it scuffs under foot traffic and dissolves where a car drips oil. Concrete floor paint is a specific product category — one-part epoxy-fortified acrylic at minimum — and substituting wall paint is money spent to repaint next year.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Recoat Windows</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Two-part and epoxy-fortified products have a window during which the second coat chemically bonds to the first. Miss it and the second coat sits on top mechanically, which is a much weaker bond and the usual cause of one layer lifting cleanly off another. Read the recoat times before you start, not after the first coat.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
